### 1.2 The lattice Ward identity (`WardTransverseVertex.lean`)

Working from the literal Wilson action rather than from an abstract vertex:

* `wilsonBgZeroMatrix_eq` — the closed form of the zero-background Wilson cubic
  vertex, `δ_{αβ} sin(2πx_ρ) − (cos(πx_ρ)/2)(δ_{βρ}p̂_α + δ_{αρ}p̂_β)`;
* **`transverseProjector_conj_wilsonBgZeroMatrix`** — `P W P = sin(2πx_ρ)·P`;
* **`transverseProjector_conj_gaugeVariationMatrix`** — `P R_gauge P = 0`.

So the constrained propagator sees exactly one scalar from the Wilson sector and
nothing at all from the gauge-projection sector.

## 2. The obstruction found (and why it matters)

`A1WardAssembly.mixedPatch_entry_hypothesis_fails` proves that the entry
hypothesis used upstream on the mixed-component patch,

    |V_{01;1}(q)|² / 2  ≤  I(q)   on   mixedPatch (1/16),

is **false** once `I` is the literal one-loop integrand.  At the point
`q = (1/4, 0, 1/2, 1/2)` — which lies in the mixed patch for every admissible
half-width — the Ward factor `sin(2πq_ρ)` vanishes, hence the literal integrand
vanishes identically, while the mixed component of the constrained first
variation is bounded below.

This is a source-identification obstruction, not an estimate that can be
sharpened: the mixed-component patch is exactly where the literal Gaussian
coefficient is blind.

**Replacement.**  The same Ward identity supplies a working box.  `wardPatch`
(`q_0 ∈ [0,1/8]`, `q_1 ∈ [1/8,1/4]`, `q_2, q_3 ∈ [0,1/2]`) has volume `1/256`,
`sin(2πq_1) ≥ 1/2` and transverse projector diagonal entry `≥ 1/2` throughout,
giving a *computed* integrand floor `I ≥ 1/32768` and hence

    β_j(g) ≥ 1/16777216 > 0        (`A1_beta_pos_ward`)

for every shell index and admissible history.

## 3. The integrability obligation, discharged

`A1_beta_pos_ward` still assumed integrability of the literal integrand.  That is
a genuine analytic obligation, and it is now a theorem.

### 3.1 Free case (`WardIntegrability.lean`)

With no Lorentz-diagonal contribution and no seagull the integrand collapses to a
scalar:

    I(q) = (3/2)·sin(2πq_ρ)² / (q̂²)²        (`literalOneLoopIntegrand_free_eq`)

using `Tr P = 3`.  Two elementary bounds — `|sin(2πt)| ≤ p̂(t)` and `p̂(t) ≥ 4t`
on `[0,1/2]` — give `I(q) ≤ (3/32)‖q‖⁻²`, and `‖q‖⁻²` is integrable in four
dimensions (`integrable_radialCutoff_norm`, via the polar-coordinates criterion).
Hence `literalOneLoopIntegrand_free_integrableOn`, and
`A1_beta_pos_ward_free` with no integrability hypothesis.

`A1_beta_pos_ward_nonvacuous` is a completely instantiated instance with no
hypotheses left at all.  It is an *inhabitation* statement about the compiler
inputs and is **not** an identification of Bałaban's objects.

### 3.2 Interacting case (`LiteralIntegrability.lean`)

Power counting on the constrained propagator `G(q) = (q̂²)⁻¹P(q)`, with
`q̂² ≥ 16‖q‖²`, gives `‖q‖⁻⁴|V|² + ‖q‖⁻²|S|`.  In four dimensions `‖q‖⁻²` is
integrable and `‖q‖⁻⁴` is not.  Therefore:

* a **bounded** seagull insertion is harmless;
* the first variation must **vanish at zero momentum**, at least linearly.

Crucially, it is the *transverse conjugate* `P V P` that must vanish linearly,
not `V` itself: the literal gauge-projection variation has entries of size
`‖q‖⁻¹` (see `gaugeVariationMatrix_eq`) and is only killed after conjugation.
This is why the naive entrywise route fails and the Ward identity is needed.

* `literalOneLoopIntegrand_integrableOn_of_conjBound` — the criterion.
* `sourceVariation_conj_bound` — the literal source vertex satisfies it, with
  constant `4π + 64·K_D`, where `K_D` is the linear-vanishing constant of the
  Lorentz-diagonal averaging/constrained-projection variation.
* `A1_beta_pos_ward_of_linearVanishing` — Row A1a from the literal one-loop
  trace, integrability discharged, for interacting source data.

The linear-vanishing requirement is not an assumption imposed for convenience:
it is the momentum-space form of `AnnihilatesZero`, the property the literal
transforms of Bałaban's averaging and constrained-projection operators are
already known to carry.

